WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.235 --> 00:00:01.860
ROGER MARTINEZ: So
you decided that you

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.560
want to move your application
or some of your infrastructure

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.260
from an on-premises
VMware vSphere environment

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.180
to Google Cloud Compute Engine.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.200
That's great.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.380
There's only one thing standing
in your way, the migration

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.195
itself.

00:00:14.195 --> 00:00:16.320
There are a lot of things
that you need to consider

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.030
when planning a migration.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.110
How do you choose which
services you need?

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.590
How will you test your
application before moving it?

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.360
How can you minimize
any downtime

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.520
associated with migration?

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.860
Luckily, Google Cloud
offers a managed service

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.625
for migrating to Compute Engine.

00:00:30.625 --> 00:00:33.000
Today, I'm going to help you
get familiar with the latest

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.220
release of Migrate
for Compute Engine.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.947
Like the name suggests,
Migrate for Compute Engine

00:00:44.947 --> 00:00:46.530
lets you migrate
your virtual machines

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.090
from your on-premises
data center

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.950
onto Google Cloud
Compute Engine.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.220
It's a managed service.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.095
So all of its functionality is
baked right into Google Cloud.

00:00:54.095 --> 00:00:56.220
That means you can manage
your migration from start

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.580
to finish from the Cloud
Console or, if you want,

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.160
you can use a cloud API for
programmatically migrating

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.300
workloads.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.129
It's got some
built-in testing that

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.560
will help you validate things
before you make the move.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.060
And there is no
client-side software

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.685
that you need to
worry about installing

00:01:10.685 --> 00:01:11.760
on your virtual machines.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.020
All of that sounds great.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.440
But moving your virtual machines
from your own on-prem racks

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.570
to Google Cloud is a big move.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.850
Let's clarify what that
process looks with Migrate

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.260
for Compute Engine.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.383
Before anything, you'll need
to create a host project.

00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:28.050
This is the Google Cloud project
that will control the migration

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.008
process.

00:01:29.008 --> 00:01:31.050
This can be the same
project that will eventually

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.232
host your migrated VMs.

00:01:32.232 --> 00:01:34.440
But you do have the option
of using a separate target

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:35.730
project.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.340
You also need to have the
Migrate Connector running

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.220
from your on-premises
vSphere environment.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.920
Migrate Connector will set your
on-premise vSphere environment

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.590
as a migration source and
establish a secure data

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.560
path between it and
your host project.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.330
This is accomplished
using Google Cloud

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.760
APIs over port 443.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.150
And the traffic can be
routed via the internet, VPN,

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.280
private Google access, or
dedicated interconnect.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.935
Once Migrate Connector is
set up in your environment,

00:02:03.935 --> 00:02:05.810
it will be able to
perform storage operations

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.139
against your VM disks, create
your on-prem VM inventory,

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:12.260
and stop and monitor
your source VMs.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.230
All of that is done
using the vSphere APIs.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:16.802
To get Migrate
Connector going, there

00:02:16.802 --> 00:02:18.260
are some prerequisites
steps you'll

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.510
need to complete, like
creating a vCenter user,

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:24.770
generating SSH keys, and
configuring network access.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:27.440
I'll include a link to the
additional setup associated

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.900
with Migrate Connector in
this video's description.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.940
Once you've got Migrate
Connector and your host

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.310
project all set up,
the migration process

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.840
can begin going
through its six phases.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.440
Phase 1, onboarding--
this is when

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.960
you'll choose which VMs
you're going to migrate.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.940
The list of virtual
machines that are available

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.500
will appear on
the console thanks

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:46.940
to the Migrate Connector.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.650
Once you choose
your machines, you

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.620
move on to phase 2, replication.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.960
During the replication
phase, the disk data

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.110
from the source VM will begin
its replication to Google Cloud

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.675
by creating VMware snapshots.

00:02:59.675 --> 00:03:01.800
This is done without having
to take the VM offline.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.200
So there's no downtime here.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.690
After the initial
replication, Compute Engine

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.240
will then perform
incremental replications.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.490
These are replications
of the disk that occur

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.620
at a regular time interval.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.260
These incremental replications
can be paused and resumed

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:16.460
as you see fit.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:19.940
As replication continues,
you move on to phase 3,

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:21.510
setting your target.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:24.170
This is when you specify
your landing zone, where

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.300
you want your VM to migrate to.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.270
The target details include
the project, instance type,

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.953
and network settings.

00:03:30.953 --> 00:03:33.120
You don't have to create a
compute instance yourself

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.590
because Migrate
for Compute Engine

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.660
will take care of
doing that for you.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.920
After setting your target,
onto phase 4, the test clone--

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.870
here's where you actually
clone your source VM

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:45.300
to a Compute Engine instance
for testing purposes

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.390
using the latest
replication data available.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.580
During this phase, you
should perform all the tests

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:51.960
and validations
that you need for

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.340
your particular application.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.170
The source VM continues
to run as well as

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.420
the incremental replications.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:01.120
The test clone is
optional, but definitely

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.380
recommended to ensure a smooth
phase 5, the cut-over phase.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:06.490
This is it.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:09.010
During the cut-over phase,
the source VM is stopped,

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:11.290
replication is
finalized and ended,

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:13.150
and a new Compute
Engine instance

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:16.500
is created on Google Cloud,
the latest replication data.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.695
This phase will cause a
short amount of downtime.

00:04:18.695 --> 00:04:20.070
So a maintenance
window will need

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.500
to be implemented while you
turn down the origin machine

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.780
and redirect traffic
to the cloud.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.610
If all goes well, you'll
be able to make it

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:30.840
to the sixth and final phase,
finalizing your migration.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.900
At this phase, all replications
data and storage resources

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.960
associated with the migration
of your VMs are deleted

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.690
and the VM state is
set to finalized.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.300
You've migrated your
VM to Compute Engine.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.250
For more details on
those six phases,

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.610
check out the documentation for
VM migration lifecycle linked

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:48.660
in the description.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:50.340
The goal of Migrate
for Compute Engine

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:53.600
is to give you a frictionless
migration experience.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.350
This is largely
accomplished by performing

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.390
agentless replication
and by leveraging

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:59.880
vSphere and Google Cloud APIs.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.220
By including the ability
to deploy test clones,

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:04.050
you're able to test
workloads before having

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:05.820
to make the big move.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.980
Best of all, the end-to-end
migration journey

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.680
is centralized in
the Cloud Console.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.510
There, in the
migration dashboard,

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:14.640
you'll be able to see all
the migrations in flight,

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.580
view migration events,
manage sources and targets,

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.160
and create VM groups so that
you can perform bulk migration

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.810
tasks on multiple VMs at once.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.892
So what do you think about
Migrate for Compute Engine?

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:27.975
I hope that my walk-through
of the migration phase

00:05:27.975 --> 00:05:30.267
has helped you determine how
Migrate for Compute Engine

00:05:30.267 --> 00:05:33.122
can help you get your on-prem
applications into the cloud.

00:05:33.122 --> 00:05:35.580
If you want to learn more about
Migrate for Compute Engine,

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:40.140
check out cloud.google.com
/migrate/computeengine,

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.390
and check out the link in
the description for a free

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:45.540
assessment to estimate
your cloud migration costs.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.550
Thank you for watching.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:50.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

